  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Thursday...\n\nA shortwave and cold front, associated with a parent area of low \npressure centered over far NW Ontario will drop into the region \nstarting late this afternoon. This will usher in cooler\nconditions, north to northeast winds, and increasing shower\nchances as a band of frontogenesis drops south into the southern\nmountains and foothills. A few thunderstorms are possible over\nnorthern areas this afternoon and evening, and CAMS do show a\ncouple helicity tracks, though for the most part, weak\nthunderstorms are anticipated. Along with the band of\nfrontogenesis, northeasterly upslope flow will develop, favoring\nthe northern aspects of the southern mountains and foothills. Look\nfor showers to increase in coverage tonight into early Saturday,\nespecially over the south. Showers decrease in coverage by\nSaturday afternoon, though with cyclonic flow in place, isolated\nshowers will linger over portions of the area to the evening. Weak\ninstability over the region may allow for a few rumbles. Precipitation\ntotals look to range from a few hundredths to a trace over the \nnorth, to between 0.50 to 1 inches of precipitation, with locally\nhigher totals over the northerly upslope regions of the southern \nmountains/foothills. There is a moderate to high chance of at \nleast 0.25 inches of precipitation south and west of a Melville to\nBillings to Alzada line. Snow levels look to fall as low as 7,500\nfeet Saturday morning, with some light snow accumulations \nexpected in the southern mountains. Greatest totals will be found \non northerly aspects above 9,000 feet. Snow will likely impact the\nBeartooth Highway, and have issued a Winter Weather Advisory, in \neffect from this evening through Saturday morning. Prepare for \nimpacts to recreation and travel in the high country.\n\nEnsembles generally depict drier northwest flow with occasional \npieces of energy bringing isolated shower and weak thunderstorm \nactivity to the region in the afternoons/evenings Sunday into\nTuesday. The threat for strong to severe thunderstorms during this\ntimeframe is very low. By late Tuesday into Wednesday, a more \nactive wave is possible with ensembles depicting energy moving the\nregion in northwest flow. Temperatures are forecast to range\nfrom the 50s and 60s Saturday, to the 60s to lower 70s Sunday.\nTemperatures then warm into the 70s to lower 80s Monday through\nThursday, though Tuesday could see temperatures climb into the\nmiddle 80s. STP",
